detail.abstract:
Multilevel selection theory (MLS2) has recently been applied to legal evolution, most notably in Fábio Portela Lopes de Almeida's work treating constitutional societies as "Darwinian individuals" subject to group-level selection.&nbsp; 
<div>
 This paper argues that MLS2 applied to legal systems reintroduces the teleological thinking that gene-centered evolutionary biology and Austrian economics rejected. I postulate that Extended Phenotype Theory (EPT), which treats legal institutions as extended phenotypes of competing memes, provides superior explanatory and predictive power through three lines of argument. First, MLS2 lacks specifiable causal mechanisms: the pathways by which "societies" supposedly compete and transmit traits remain obscure. Second, parsimony favors EPT: Occam's razor cuts against multiplying levels of selection when lower-level mechanisms suffice. Third, empirical tests consistently favor EPT over MLS2.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Four case studies of dysfunctional norms show EPT predicting persistence with 100% accuracy while MLS2 predicts collapse. Quantitative metrics derived from EPT (Constitutional Lock-in Index, IusMorfos) generate falsifiable predictions with 78% accuracy, while MLS2 offers only vague claims about "fitter societies." Genealogical analysis reveals 91% of constitutional norms have ancestors predating supposed "punctuational" events, undermining MLS2's revolutionary narrative.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 EPT tries to advance legal evolution from philosophy to falsifiable science by grounding institutional analysis in memetic competition rather than group-level teleology.
</div>
